{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation design settled after reading ADR 0112 + the existing admission seam (recorded here rather than in the description, which is under the 8,000-char plan-review admission cap).\n\n**Seam.** `rebar.llm.peak_rss.gate_peak_rss` is already a context manager wrapping exactly the scope S6 needs, and its own docstring states the reason: \"Wrapping the library entry points (`review_plan` / `verify_completion`) rather than the MCP daemon and the CLI separately covers both call paths at one seam, since both reach the gate through these functions.\" Admission takes the same seam — a sibling context manager in `rebar.llm.gate_admission`, entered immediately outside `gate_peak_rss` in `review_plan()` and `verify_completion()`. One wrapper, both gates, both AC4 paths, one shared counter.\n\n**Counter.** N slot files under the snapshot store's existing `locks/` directory (`store_root()/locks/gate-slot-<i>`), each held with a non-blocking `fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)` for the duration of the run and released in a `finally`. This is cross-process (AC4: CLI runs are separate processes) and crash-safe by construction (AC5: the kernel drops a flock when the holding process dies or its fd closes — no PID heartbeat, which ADR 0005/the janitor already rejected as unsound). It also serialises correctly between threads of one process, because a `flock` is held by the open file description and each acquisition `open()`s its own. It inherits `REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR`, so it follows gate scratch onto ADR 0112 decision 3's dedicated volume for free.\n\n**Refusal.** `GateCongestedError` — an exception, never a verdict value, so it cannot be mistaken for INDETERMINATE or a BLOCK (AC3). CLI maps it to **exit 11**, the code this repo already documents as \"transient — retry\", rather than minting a new one. MCP returns a structured `{error: ..., retryable: true}` payload in the same shape `_structured_llm_failure` already produces, so a driving agent branches on `retryable` instead of parsing English.\n\n**Metric (AC7).** A one-line `GATE_CONGESTED {json}` stderr marker, copying the `GATE_PEAK_RSS` / `VOTER_ERROR` / `MERGE_CHANGE_ERROR` convention verbatim. That convention exists precisely because the containers hold no AWS credentials and the host probe greps journald; it is also the portable choice (`project.portability`) — it works identically under the CLI on a laptop with no CloudWatch and no CI provider.\n\n**Config (AC6).** `[snapshot].max_concurrent_gates`, resolved by a new `resolve_gate_max_concurrent` alongside `resolve_gate_min_free_bytes`. `0` disables the bound (the off-switch idiom `max_bytes`/`max_entries` already use); a malformed value falls back to the default rather than crashing every gate, matching `resolve_gate_min_free_bytes`'s stated posture.\n\n**Deliberate deviation from ADR 0112 decision 6 — no `REBAR_GATE_*` env tier.** The ADR asks for `REBAR_GATE_* env > [snapshot] > default`. The env tier is omitted. `scripts/_mechanism_delta/detect_config.py` censuses `REBAR_*` literals as the `env_var` kind, so a new `REBAR_GATE_MAX_CONCURRENT` is a new mechanism — and bug `a1f1-a30d-2d50-4f5a` records that a new mechanism is currently **unlandable**: a `# mechanism-ok:` marker satisfies `check_mechanism_delta.py --check` but `tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py::test_check_passes_on_the_committed_tree` asserts `new == 0` regardless of markers, and every route to the baseline is closed to a contributor. The `[snapshot]` table itself is a `_RESERVED_SECTIONS` member and so is not in `_SECTIONS`, which is what the `config_key`/`feature_flag` detectors read — confirmed against `.github/mechanism-baseline.json`, which holds zero `config_key::snapshot.*` entries. The config tier therefore lands cleanly today and the env tier can be added in one line once `a1f1` is fixed. Filed here so the gap is a recorded decision, not an oversight.\n\n**Default = 4.** Derived from the measurements this epic already took, not guessed. Per-gate resident cost: plan_review `peak_rss_bytes` ~748 MB, verify_completion ~462 MB (`GATE_PEAK_RSS` markers; ADR 0112 cites ~739 MB / ~4.7 min, and a degraded run at 501 MB, so the figure varies run to run). The host is a `t4g.large` (8 GiB) shared with Gerrit, and ADR 0112 decision 7 forbids growing it. Steady state is ~2.17 GB and Gerrit reserves ~3 GiB by config (`heapLimit 2g` + `packedGitLimit 1g`), leaving roughly 3 GiB for concurrent gate work. 4 x 748 MB ~= 2.99 GB fits inside that; a fifth concurrent run (~3.74 GB) does not. 4 is also decisively below the operator's stated ~10 concurrent plan reviews, which is the point — a cap at or above observed peak sheds no load and bounds nothing."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788491645519320001,"uuid":"798e8066-c24f-4a3a-928e-b679b45155cb"}